-- js-callgraph.rofl — the CLAIMS the call-graph model makes about itself.
-- Rules derive; this file asserts, and every assertion here is answerable by
-- `npm run report` rather than by reading prose.
--
-- Three ledgers, and they are not interchangeable:
--
--   node_kind / handled / ignored / unknown_because   — js-model's matrix,
--       keyed by babel node KIND x layer. Modelling the call graph made this
--       layer touch kinds nobody had declared; they are declared here, and
--       the count of them is the price of the cell.
--   shape_because                                     — keyed by callee SHAPE,
--       which is the dimension the kind matrix cannot express. `member_expression`
--       HAS a rule and still misses seven of its eight shapes; at kind level that
--       cell reads `modelled` and the residue is invisible.
--   checked                                           — evidence, not claim.

-- ===========================================================================
-- KINDS THE CALL GRAPH DRAGGED IN
--
-- MEASURED, not guessed: `kind_undeclared[audit](K)` derives the kinds this
-- layer's rules touch and `node_kind(js, K)` does not declare. Before this
-- block it reported 17; after it, 0. Those 17 are new rows in a matrix that is
-- LAYER-WIDE, so each one also opens an unanswered dataflow cell — a call-graph
-- rule is what put `this_expression` on the dataflow queue.
node_kind(js, identifier).
node_kind(js, member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_call_expression).
node_kind(js, new_expression).
-- a TS cast in the callee position, named by the classifier since 2026-09-04
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).
node_kind(js, this_expression).
node_kind(js, super).
node_kind(js, array_expression).
node_kind(js, object_expression).
node_kind(js, object_method).
node_kind(js, object_property).
node_kind(js, function_expression).
node_kind(js, arrow_function_expression).
node_kind(js, class_method).
node_kind(js, conditional_expression).
node_kind(js, sequence_expression).
node_kind(js, tsnon_null_expression).
-- the PROPERTY of a computed callee is read by the rules too, so its kinds are
-- part of the layer's vocabulary
node_kind(js, string_literal).
node_kind(js, numeric_literal).
node_kind(js, template_literal).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE RULES ACTUALLY DO — one rule id, r_call_edge, because one rule set
-- is what was written and splitting it into invented ids would be the
-- tautology js-model.rofl already paid for.
handled(js, identifier,                callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, optional_call_expression,  callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, function_expression,       callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_method,             callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_property,           callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_expression,         callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, class_method,              callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, variable_declarator,       callgraph, r_call_edge).

-- `member_expression` is the honest half-truth of this file. Tier 2 resolves
-- `ns.method()` where ns is a local object literal, so a rule exists and the
-- cell is `modelled` — and seven of that kind's eight shapes resolve nothing.
-- The kind matrix has no way to say so; the shape ledger below does.
handled(js, member_expression,         callgraph, r_call_edge).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, WITH A TYPED REASON. js-model's default for a cell
-- nobody touched is already `not_yet`; these are written out anyway, because a
-- default that is never contradicted is indistinguishable from an oversight.
unknown_because(js, this_expression,         callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, super,                   callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04: the constructor edge is modelled, and the kind is split
-- by the refinement column, so a KIND-level reason has no cell to sit on. The
-- residue that remains is `s_new_result` — `(new C())()` — and it takes the
-- default reason like every other unmodelled shape.
unknown_because(js, optional_member_expression, callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, conditional_expression,  callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, sequence_expression,     callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, tsnon_null_expression,   callgraph, not_yet).
-- the receiver is a host literal and its methods belong to the runtime, not to
-- any corpus this analyser can be pointed at
unknown_because(js, array_expression,        callgraph, out_of_scope).

-- A LITERAL AS A COMPUTED KEY. `string_literal` and `numeric_literal` are read
-- for their KIND, which is all a shape needs, and their value is on the facts
-- when a later tier wants it. `template_literal` is the one that is NOT
-- recoverable: TemplateElement.value is `{raw, cooked}` and the contract emits
-- scalar own properties only, so the chunk's text never becomes a fact. The
-- key is knowable and not derivable, which is ours and unfinished.
handled(js, string_literal,  callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, numeric_literal, callgraph, r_call_edge).
unknown_because(js, template_literal, callgraph, not_yet).

-- ===========================================================================
-- CONTROL TRANSFERS THAT ARE NOT CALL EXPRESSIONS.
--
-- This is the frontier the callee-shape taxonomy cannot see, because it starts
-- from CallExpression and these are not one. Each of them runs a function the
-- call graph never records, and the execution oracle proves it: `new Box(1)`
-- put `useClass -> Box` on the runtime's list and nowhere on the model's.
-- Declared here so the gap is a queryable row rather than a paragraph.
node_kind(js, tagged_template_expression).
node_kind(js, await_expression).
node_kind(js, for_of_statement).
node_kind(js, decorator).

unknown_because(js, tagged_template_expression, callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, await_expression,           callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, for_of_statement,           callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, decorator,                  callgraph, not_yet).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FRONTIER, BY SHAPE. Every shape whose sites do not all resolve has a row
-- here; `shape_unexplained[audit]` is the gate that says so, and it must be
-- empty. `runtime_dependent` appears EXACTLY ONCE, and that is the point of
-- the taxonomy: a computed callee `o[k]()` with a non-literal k names a method
-- that does not exist until the program runs, and nothing else on this list
-- can borrow that excuse. `a.b.c()` is merely unfinished.
shape_because(js, s_computed_dynamic_key,  callgraph, runtime_dependent).

-- ours, and on the queue
shape_because(js, s_identifier,            callgraph, not_yet).
-- FOUR REASONS RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND THE AUDIT IS WHAT DEMANDED IT.
-- `s_member_on_ident`, `s_member_on_member`, `s_member_on_this` and
-- `s_optional_member` used to say `not_yet` and every site of all four now
-- resolves through `denotes` — so `shape_stale[audit]`, the check for an excuse
-- outliving its cause, named them by name the moment tier 4 landed. Deleting
-- them is not optimism: an excuse for a shape whose sites all resolve is
-- decoration, and if a corpus ever brings back a receiver the entries do not
-- reach — an import, a parameter, a host global — the residue returns and
-- `shape_unexplained[audit]` demands a reason again. The verdict follows the
-- corpus rather than the grammar HERE, which is the opposite of the shape
-- vocabulary above, and the two audits are what keep the difference honest.
shape_because(js, s_computed_literal_key,  callgraph, not_yet).

-- `s_non_null` IS TICKED AND STILL HAS RESIDUE, which is what this table is
-- for and `unknown_because` is not: the cell is modelled — `notNull!(n)`
-- resolves through the value layer — while `maybe!(n)` does not, because
-- `maybe` is an AMBIENT declaration and an ambient declaration has no value in
-- the program by definition. That is out_of_scope rather than not_yet: the
-- implementation is elsewhere, not undone. Note the division of labour the two
-- tables have: `shape_because` describes the RESIDUE OF A SHAPE and may stand
-- beside a tick; `unknown_because` describes an UNMODELLED CELL and may not.
shape_because(js, s_non_null,              callgraph, out_of_scope).
-- knowable at parse time, and absent from the fact vocabulary: see
-- `template_literal` above. NOT runtime_dependent.
shape_because(js, s_computed_template_key, callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04 with the value core: `mkBox(n).peek()` resolves through
-- the callee's RETURN, so this shape has no residue left and `shape_stale`
-- said so. It was the cell the queue recorded as waiting on the dataflow layer.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04: `super.m()` resolves — `super` inside a class method
-- denotes the SUPERCLASS, which is one relation over the class's `super_class`
-- child. `shape_stale[audit]` named this row the moment the rule landed, for
-- the fourth time this session.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND NEITHER NEEDED A RULE. `new C().m()` and
-- `({a(){}}).a()` were `not_yet` because the corpus had no site for them, and
-- whether the rules covered them was a guess. Two sites in shapes.ts turned the
-- guess into a row: both resolve through `may_be_node` exactly as predicted —
-- a fresh instance reaches its class, an object literal is its own value.
-- `shape_stale[audit]` then named both. The excuse was about the CORPUS and
-- read as if it were about the MODEL, which is the difference a site settles
-- and an argument cannot.
-- `"str".toUpperCase()` and the like: the receiver is a primitive and the
-- method lives on its PROTOTYPE, which is the standard library rather than the
-- program. Same verdict as `s_member_on_array` and for the same reason — this
-- model has no notion of a built-in prototype, and inventing one is a
-- different programme, not a missing rule.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_literal,     callgraph, out_of_scope).
shape_because(js, s_member_on_other,       callgraph, not_yet).
shape_because(js, s_new_result,            callgraph, not_yet).
shape_because(js, s_super,                 callgraph, not_yet).
shape_because(js, s_dynamic_import,        callgraph, not_yet).

-- the receiver is a host literal — see array_expression above
shape_because(js, s_member_on_array,       callgraph, out_of_scope).

-- A shape nothing produced. It exists so that a grammar form nobody
-- anticipated is a ROW rather than a silence, and the day it appears the
-- verdict is already `not_yet` instead of nothing at all.
shape_because(js, s_unclassified,          callgraph, not_yet).

-- ===========================================================================
-- EVIDENCE. Three fixture files: two executed against a V8 stack-trace oracle,
-- one scanned only, because the grammar shapes it carries cannot be spelled in
-- runnable JS. The fixture count is the executed pair — the number the oracle
-- actually ran.
checked(js, call_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge, o_run_with_stack, 2).
